-- allium: 3
-- tx.allium
--
-- The connection-level transaction model of XTDB: the client-facing transaction
-- lifecycle that sits ABOVE db.allium. Where db.allium begins at submitTx/executeTx
-- (the Database boundary), this spec covers everything a client does before that —
-- begin / buffer / commit, access-mode resolution, and the read basis a transaction's
-- reads observe.
--
-- Scope: one client connection's transaction lifecycle.
-- Entity/field names match the Kotlin in core/src/main/kotlin/xtdb/api/Xtdb.kt
-- (Connection, Transaction, Phase Pending/Reading/Writing, Characteristics, DmlBuffer) and the SQL
-- classifier in core/src/main/kotlin/xtdb/query/SqlParser.kt → ParsedStatement.
-- Rust session state and its service boundary are in rust/crates/node/src/session.rs.
-- Rust SQL control values, parsing, and shared execution are in rust/crates/{plan,sql,node}/src/control.rs.
-- The native pgwire protocol state is in rust/crates/pgwire/src/protocol.rs.
-- Native Flight SQL handles and captured-basis execution are in rust/crates/flight-sql/src/{state,lease,execute,actions}.rs.
-- Native embedded ADBC and Clojure adapters are in rust/crates/ffi and bindings/jvm.
-- Embedded ADBC copies bound Arrow buffers into native allocations and releases the foreign buffers before returning.
-- Prepared Flight SQL and embedded ADBC queries can bind an Arrow batch row by position.
-- Scalar evaluation reads a bound parameter from its Arrow column when the expression reaches it.
-- A prepared Flight SQL query compacts the first row of a multi-row parameter batch for the streaming cursor.
-- A prepared embedded ADBC query that only projects compatible bindings uses the Arrow columns of its single argument row.
-- The embedded logical relation entry point captures the same connection read basis as a SQL query.
-- Logical relation cursors expose the root operator's pages as separate batches.
-- Logical relation queries use Arrow vectors for semantic pages, including pages that downstream operators observe before transport batching.
-- Flight SQL result batches retain the Arrow buffers of columns that already match the declared field, even when other columns require conformance.
-- An Arrow client result union of one data leg and nullable null legs conforms to that leg's declared plain field by Arrow position.
-- A fixed-size vector result column already in its declared layout keeps its component buffer.
-- Ordinary native SQL and XTQL queries and pgwire result portals retain compatible Arrow query batches while gathering the configured number of output rows.
-- A pgwire portal encodes supported primitive and timestamp cells from their Arrow positions, including active dense union legs.
-- A pgwire portal can encode numeric coefficients, standard PostgreSQL arrays, and structured JSON, JSON-LD, and Transit results from their Arrow cells.
-- The embedded Transit row cursor writes the typed wire representation from Arrow cell positions.
-- A prepared Flight SQL query that only projects compatible bindings selects the first row from their Arrow columns.
-- A Flight SQL statement ticket retains its plan, Arrow parameters, catalog and clock behind a bounded opaque handle.
-- The authenticated session and original attachment authorize redemption of its ticket.
-- Compatible list and struct parameters stay in Arrow through that projection.
-- Arithmetic on compatible integer and floating-point parameter columns uses the Arrow projection kernel.
-- Supported comparisons, casts, and string functions can consume bound Arrow columns in the vector expression evaluator.
-- When a bound Boolean or nullable buffer needs normalization, the projection copies selected cells within Arrow.
-- Compatible nested fields and union legs retain their value buffers when encoding changes their declared layout.
-- Columnar UNION ALL preserves duplicates and forwards batches in input order.
-- A columnar sort on input fields orders batch and row positions using the SQL key comparator.
-- The sort gathers payload columns by position and uses Arrow IPC for its spill runs.
-- A sort can compare supported key types directly at selected Arrow positions.
-- A sort can compute a supported key expression in its Arrow input batches and keep its payloads columnar through ordering and spill.
-- A sort can combine computed keys with keys taken directly from its input columns.
-- Its Arrow spill records retain compatible physical payload layouts.
-- Its spill threshold accounts for Arrow buffers held by selected payloads.
-- A materialized relation evaluates on its first reached reference and preserves the order of its pages.
-- Native execution holds its pages within the materialization row and memory bounds.
-- When either bound is crossed it replays all pages through one query-scoped spill file, using the column spill format.
-- Every reference has its own replay position and every outstanding page retains its own memory.
-- Measured operators reserve against sibling scopes of the same query budget.
-- Each operator can report its own retained-allocation peak and the query reports the peak across all operators.
-- EXPLAIN ANALYZE measures the opened Arrow operators when the query uses Arrow execution.
-- A direct Arrow parameter projection charges its own operator scope when traced.
-- Native query spans use the application name of their connection.
-- Pgwire uses the application name supplied at startup or by SET.
-- Flight SQL connections default to XTDB Flight SQL and embedded connections default to XTDB.
-- The application name survives DISCARD ALL.
-- Those spill pages retain each record's fields and preserve their original page boundaries.
-- Computed VALUES and replayed literal pages admit their results column by column.
-- Column tables preserve one semantic page across the input list's batches.
-- Numeric list expression pages retain typed child arrays and validity through the list cursor.
-- A cosine projection guarded by a simple Boolean CASE reads only the rows selected by its guard.
-- A list cast to REAL or DOUBLE PRECISION elements converts the packed components of a numeric list in one pass, through binary64 as each scalar cast does.
-- A packed numeric list derives its observed element type from the first selected occurrence of each present leg and of null.
-- Native list pages can cast numeric, text, and supported temporal components directly into their result child buffers.
-- A page applies the conversion and projection checks in component order.
-- A list parameter enters evaluation as its Arrow cell.
-- Score calculation and nullable result construction use Arrow input arrays and output buffers.
-- The integer series cursor writes the generated page directly into a signed 64-bit Arrow vector.
-- Computed UNNEST gathers both original payload and evaluated child columns by Arrow position.
-- Its ordinal vector marks the synthetic outer row of an empty list as null.
-- Query analysis measures a batch-capable operator using its emitted Arrow batches.
-- A batch join can gather matching payload positions from selected Arrow vectors.
-- A compatible inner equijoin hashes and compares its Arrow key cells at their build and probe positions.
-- Its Bloom and entity ID filters use those same build-side vectors.
-- An equijoin can partition and replay its consumed input as Arrow record batches and Arrow selections.
-- Supported computed keys and integer residual comparisons retain the payload positions through match selection.
-- Compatible semi and anti equijoins select their output from the probe vectors and emit each selected probe position once.
-- Their columnar output keeps the configured transport row limit.
-- Compatible outer equijoins select both sides by position and use null positions for unmatched rows.
-- A single equijoin fails when more than one build row matches a probe row.
-- A columnar cross join gathers pairs of positions from its two inputs in left-major order within each batch pair.
-- Correlated Apply retains independent Arrow payload positions and binds direct column arguments as Arrow cells.
-- Apply can append dependent rows, Boolean marks, records, and lists of records to those positions.
-- Relation ordinality and unpartitioned ROW_NUMBER with supported ordering append a signed 64-bit ordinal vector to Arrow batch inputs.
-- Unpartitioned LEAD and LAG gather shifted cells from their ordered Arrow input and append null cells at the window boundary.
-- A partitioned window over compatible Arrow keys assigns partition identifiers in first-seen order and keeps shifts within those partitions.
-- The embedded logical relation entry point runs plan reporting and measured execution on that read basis.
-- The authenticated history position orders decisions, and the operation ordinal orders effects inside a decision.
-- SQL system time is nondecreasing and can tie.
-- The receiver judges live preconditions with a fresh certified reading of its installed independent clock domain.
-- A deadline is exclusive.
-- A funded turn can bind an attempt to an advertised grid reading and a half-open service window.
-- A missed window publishes no effects at a replacement reading and leaves an uncertain original attempt to settlement.
-- Typed create-only document inserts check absence over their valid-time intervals in the ordered transaction view.
-- An overlap aborts the entire transaction.
-- Owned native inserts resolve in the authenticated principal's versioned namespace.
-- The owner declares the first layout before an owned relation has stored data.
-- A version 1 declaration is immutable and specifies one scalar type per field.
-- The owned Flight capability action advertises version 2 and a separate declaration action admits that version.
-- The layout lookup returns the selected Root relation's declaration version at an authenticated live cut and includes the effective declaration for version 2.
-- Version 2 admits bounded clear scalar, dense-union, list and struct shapes.
-- An owned Flight upload aligns dense-union offset buffers before decoding a retained message slice.
-- Each admitted Decimal128 coefficient fits the precision of its selected leaf, including inside lists and structs.
-- An ordered version 2 redeclaration joins the new clear shapes with the stored declaration and preserves earlier admissions.
-- A field whose joined shape exceeds its bound admits canonical Binary alongside its retained shape.
-- Installed path columns keep their original types and presence obligations.
-- Opaque field kinds are fixed and excluded from owned paths.
-- An owned create or replacement enforces that schema at the ordering cut.
-- A creation-lifetime relation declares its immutable acceptance extent before any fact exists.
-- Its original ordered creation fixes the exclusive expiry and reserves the full identity across that expiry.
-- A live point uses the live admission clock reading and reports the fact's exclusive expiry.
-- Retained, archived and other read routes without lifetime coverage refuse the affected relation.
-- An acceptance-valid root declaration requires every create, replacement and deletion to begin at its original receiver decision reading and have no valid end.
-- Source encoding preserves whether an owned field was omitted or explicitly null.
-- A live placed held read can resolve one installed ordinary control point under the hosted writer's allowlist.
-- The control lookup proves an epoch-to-infinity slice or covered absence and returns its originally held revision for a checked commit.
-- Owned deadlines and point observations are checked at the ordered cut before owned mutations apply.
-- A present point identifies the original transaction position and event ordinal.
-- A Root publication selected for a fork retains the winning marker's position through archive choice, suffix replay and landing.
-- An owned archive scan at that Root publication excludes transactions above its position even when their SQL history times tie.
-- Applicable erasure continues to govern the selected historical winner.
-- Equal SQL system times do not make two owned assertions interchangeable.
-- One grouped owned Flight commit supplies one deadline and batches for distinct relations.
-- With no relation batches, the commit validates the deadline at its ordered position and changes no owned relation.
-- Observations across those relations share the ordered cut, and their mutations apply atomically in group and row order.
-- Owned create, replace, delete, and erase operations apply atomically in their listed order.
-- Owned erase requires a verified permanent root identity and emits an all-history erase event.
-- An erase advances the owner's retained-read fence and the identity's historical release floor.
-- Purge floors compare complete typed handles inside protected buckets. Digest collisions share a bucket without sharing a floor.
-- The exact floor index covers the same ordered relation erase frontier. Missing coverage refuses historical release.
-- Owned erase refuses governed paths and another mutation of the same identity in the commit.
-- Owned point reads compare the complete stored identity before yielding any row.
-- An owned transaction refuses ordinary operations and ordinary SQL catalogs and scans exclude owned tables.
-- Owned point reads use the principal's namespace at the admitted exact cut.
-- Ordered owned mutations reserve permanent addresses using the canonical IID and deterministic collision probes.
-- A protected registry records each locator's complete typed identity with the fact write.
-- Point reads and ordered checks open the registry at the same cut as the fact and verify its complete stored identity.
-- An existing relation without a complete registry refuses owned access.
-- A held owned read captures the exact applied transaction key and retains that catalog for repeated reads.
-- An ordinary unscoped held Root read can prove the absent prefix of up to 64 ordered keys on one relation at a common valid instant.
-- The prefix proof retains the held system and requested valid coordinates and admits only relations without a creation lifetime.
-- A caller may submit the original per-key observations and create operations together in one ordered commit batch.
-- The commit checks every observation at the ordering cut and publishes the page atomically.
-- A placed hosted import decision may carry one Arrow batch for several ordered runs on the same Root relation.
-- Its schedule restores each run at its original position among inline effects and reconstructs field presence before ordering.
-- An ordinary Flight caller can submit a bounded ordered sequence of held point or range requests in one action.
-- Each entry uses its scalar action's admission, held cut, authorization and delivery fence.
-- The owner streams one scalar IPC reply per successful entry and terminates at the first refusal with that entry's scalar status.
-- Held selections carry ordered UTF-8 points and valid windows on their original handles with each entry's original cut.
-- Compatible point selections reuse the Arrow field schema and retain reply metadata per entry.
-- Reserved audience point selections spend one original action and work allowance per entry and preserve the original route and reply limits.
-- Fresh Flight readers can batch the Root selectors admitted by their route.
-- An ordinary Flight caller can opt into Arrow IPC message framing for a raw owned reply.
-- A historical point uses the held catalog and the same identity registry within the admitted cut and its requested system-time cap.
-- The relation journal certifies historical point coverage, and incomplete coverage refuses the read.
-- A covered held range may mint a witness of its owner relation's cumulative event frontier and placed writer epoch.
-- A declared ordered path on an empty relation validates bounds against the held schema and has exact zero cardinality before the first fact.
-- An ordered read can extend a checkpoint with a bounded exact replay of the held relation's flushed and live events.
-- A replay that lacks complete source coverage or exceeds its bound refuses the read.
-- Ordered validation refuses a witnessed write when that relation's frontier changed or the witness expired or belongs to another placed epoch.
-- Events in other owner relations leave the witness valid.
-- An owned root mutation appends its owner relation's next dense change group in the same committed transaction.
-- The first tracked group shares its transaction with the relation's feed floor.
-- A held feed page seeks by exclusive position and returns complete groups in transaction order.
-- Its coverage ends at the last returned group when truncated or at the admitted cut when exhausted.
-- A regional held feed retains touches whose complete identities and valid extents intersect the requested region.
-- It advances coverage over all source groups it examined and can return an empty page with an advanced cursor.
-- A regional held world feed selects one admitted local relation and anchors that coverage to the held world and the requested upper cut.
-- An empty relation is covered through the admitted cut, and a request crossing untracked events or a missing group is refused.
-- A held merged feed seeks the selected owner relations at one cut and groups their visible touches by original transaction position.
-- A capped page publishes only whole visible commits, and a nonempty page resumes at its last visible commit.
-- An owned root relation may declare a cap for each distinct fixed-length prefix of its binary fact key.
-- A populated relation can initialize its first cap when validity always begins at acceptance and has an open end.
-- The declaration initializes each covered counter from the transaction's final visible membership at its ordering point.
-- A prefix already above the proposed maximum aborts the declaration and the transaction's other writes.
-- Redeclaration can change a projection's maximum while retaining its bucket identities and coverage. Tightening checks every bucket against the transaction's final state.
-- A hosted Root commit can declare capacity alongside catalog effects, guarded by the checked installation authority.
-- An admitted hosted PgWire catalog transaction can submit the same capacity effect against the installed writer's exact revision. Capacity validation and catalog writes share the final ordered outcome.
-- The ordered resolver validates the final prefix memberships and commits covered counter deltas with the current-valid fact changes.
-- A hosted Root commit can retire owned Root relations alongside its catalog effects.
-- Retirement records the relation, its retirement transaction and a retention of at most one hundred years in the principal's retired catalog.
-- It deletes the ordered, search and spatial paths naming the relation from their catalogs, and keeps its declaration for historical reads within the retention.
-- Every later write, declaration or path naming a retired relation aborts its transaction, as does any other operation on a relation the same transaction retires.
-- Retiring again with the same retention changes nothing; another retention conflicts.
-- A grouped owned commit may bound multiple complete declared Binary prefixes at its ordered-present valid instant.
-- Before applying any mutation, the resolver validates each declared prefix and checks coverage against its ordered relation frontier.
-- It compares every bound with the corresponding covered counter after applying the commit's membership changes.
-- A checked Proposal settlement binds a count condition to its own world and evaluates the complete counted prefix across the retained parent and final overlay.
-- A positioned Proposal landing may use a signed Root bound adjusted by the reviewed suffix.
-- The private Proposal slot carries the held Root's through-position and a separate receiver-clock target.
-- A positioned receiver evaluates the half-open slot using its independent clock observation.
-- The receiver derives the staged generation's event position from the admitted source command.
-- The receiver derives its SQL history coordinate independently of the clock offer.
-- Prepared effects and terminal metadata install only under the exact assigned source position and predecessor.
-- Committed clock-slot receipts retain the original offered reading and through-position with the receiver sample.
-- The clock target must be at or before source append.
-- An ordered receipt reads the assigned transaction position from its winning assertion after publication.
-- Attempt recovery can join that position to its original receiver observation on the retained Root cut.
-- A placed SQL snapshot token seals its journal generation and checkpoint digest.
-- After restart, the reader can rebuild the exact retained cut from that checkpoint and its protected suffix.
-- Ordered readmission protects the selected published checkpoint and its exact suffix in one journal decision.
-- It opens the checkpoint's sealed manifest under that hold and admits physical suffix files from applicable replayed records.
-- An inaccessible checkpoint member cannot be replaced by a similarly named object.
-- Suffix replay retains custody through answer release and refuses a missing interval or a different installed data head.
-- An erased replay interval reports erasure.
-- An installed finite-period FactStore relation can count its complete owned membership at the receiver's valid-time cut.
-- In a Proposal, local period rows and absence shadows mask the same identity in the held parent at that cut.
-- An installed sample projection folds the complete assertion population per series in accepted order and counts each surviving sample coordinate once.
-- A later retraction removes a point or span and a later append establishes membership again.
-- For a reviewed landing prefix the receiver admits exclusions only for later sample assertions carried by the candidate.
-- Fixed-label protected count guards use the complete carrier identity population with ordered row or unit fences and row expiry metadata.
-- The protected membership projection reads no carrier body.
-- When a registered decision offers a refusal receipt, a violated count or constraint selects that receipt and its exact attempt in the same order.
-- The refusal retains the original Proposal settlement receipt without a new overlay root or approval, and charges the retained bytes of the selected business receipt.
-- A world overlay may withdraw a fact under a current Root capacity or uniqueness rule.
-- A replacement outside every current Root capacity predicate does not increase a covered count.
-- In placed settlement, an acceptance-valid world write checks the current Root capacity against its final overlay and exact retained parent.
-- The receiver opens each frozen writer under its registered pin at the exact source cut.
-- From newest to oldest, a tombstone or replacement masks older members with the same identity.
-- Capacity compares the complete effective populations on either side of the local write.
-- A growth beyond the current maximum aborts the world writes and accompanying effects.
-- A Root unique claim covers the fact's exact valid interval.
-- The ordered resolver checks the final holders of each affected key over every changed interval.
-- Adjacent claims may use the same key, and an overlapping claim aborts the transaction.
-- A unique point resolves the holder at the same valid instant as the returned fact.
-- Held replay certifies the locator, interval and holder of every derived unique occupancy change.
-- A world unique claim checks final local occupancy and the exact archived occupant for its path and key.
-- A final local shadow can withdraw an inherited occupant and transfer the unique key in the same ordered write.
-- A finite Proposal or Held world validates each affected unique key over its final winning local intervals and the exact admitted archived parent.
-- Newer shadows mask an inherited claim only for the same entity and the intersection with their winning valid intervals.
-- For each ancestor or Root holder, the receiver checks whether the union of newer masks covers its overlap with a local claim.
-- Two adjacent half-open claims may share the key, and any remaining intersection aborts the world write.
-- Acceptance-valid writes in a Held world start at the logical clock that the world publishes with them and have an open end.
-- Held Original replay installs complete-key capacity rules at their source transaction and requires the exact resulting frontier and counter effects.
-- When an Original replay receipt carries archived assertion positions, the governed events retain those exact positions in the destination transaction.
-- A fresh held read can derive a scoped read-only hold at the actual last visible publication marker.
-- Each declared relation's retained floor covers both the requested and selected system coordinates.
-- The derived hold shares the original generation and lease and admits point, valid-window, version, ordered page and count reads in its fixed root scope.
-- Historical ordered pages and counts use a fully covered index revision at the admitted system cut and explicit valid instant.
-- Historical index coverage includes ordered erasures, and historical pages and counts carry no commit evidence.

-- A declared fixed-valid Binary and Utf8 identity path journals both old and new covered keys for each assertion at the authored instant, including assertions shadowed later in the same commit.
-- A held full-span fixed witness permits intervening relation writes only when every covered journal side lies outside its bounds.
-- Missing exact evidence leaves relation-frontier validation in force.
-- A placed scoped point read binds its complete entity, original basis, scope and deadline, including on absence.
-- The ordered check certifies every group after the held basis and refuses a write when any assertion intersects that identity and valid interval.
-- A scoped point read whose logical clock can move holds its identity over all valid time.
-- An explicit fixed point bounds its ordered evidence to the selected microsecond.
-- A completed fixed-window read at held head binds the exact identity and requested valid interval, including gaps.
-- An admitted placed hosted writer may replace checked ordinary control rows with owned root facts in one ordered transaction.
-- Exact control identities and expected revisions are checked at the ordered base before either set of writes.
-- A failed control or fact operation publishes neither set of effects.
-- A hosted Root publication may include read conditions issued by placed held worlds in the same attachment.
-- At ordering, each condition checks its original world pin, Root purge frontier and observed local point or relation frontier.
-- The transaction orders these conditions with Root facts and hosted controls, and the observed worlds receive no writes.
-- Held reads require the original session and attachment and refuse after expiry, lease loss, or closure.
-- At the admitted system cut, a held root valid window returns ordered non-overlapping winning slices clipped at the requested endpoints.
-- A held position point or valid window excludes assertions from the first masked position through the end of one commit before resolving the winners.
-- A Root point or valid window reports the stored ordinal of each positioned winner through its owned read response.
-- An ordered index keeps separate revision roots for tied historical times and applies the held cut through its original transaction.
-- Rebuilding an ordered index suppresses assertions before the full erasure position, including earlier assertions at the same historical time.
-- A held position read still honours live ordered erasure and returns no commit evidence.
-- A held world valid window can resolve the archived parent alone for a relation without a fixed lifetime.
-- Its ordered condition checks the original parent pin and Root purge frontier.
-- Finite Proposal replacements can land after the Root publication advances when the transaction checks each exact start and both held timeline frontiers for each affected relation.
-- A registered decision must tie each partition frontier marker to a write to that Root relation, with one marker per relation and one offered control write per address.
-- A landing with point writes requires the original parent publication and may also contain finite replacements at distinct addresses.
-- A Proposal overlay may append multiple fresh carriers in any generation.
-- Each create checks its own original absence in the parent and overlay.
-- The transaction checks a Root absence and original Proposal winner for each insert and rejects an identity with retained history or a purge floor.
-- Fully covered range counts and completed head valid windows can issue relation-frontier evidence under the same placed lease as range pages.
-- Held system versions seek both window edges in the relation's ordered change journal and verify its event frontier.
-- Both directions advance past every scanned source group, including groups with no change to the requested identity.
-- A placed read can receive a conservative relation witness after rendering the complete version page.
-- A historical hold uses the original journal to certify version pages through its selected cut and issues no commit evidence.
-- Each admitted version resolves the complete valid timeline at that revision on the same pinned physical catalog.
-- An owned path declaration is maintained from the ordered owner's private catalog.
-- An owned unique declaration starts before any relation data and admits one Binary key per row.
-- Every ordered unique mutation commits the covered relation frontier together with its occupancy changes.
-- A held current-valid unique lookup requires the declaration frontier to cover the captured relation.
-- A hit proves both the full occupancy identity and the winning fact holder's complete Binary key.
-- A covered miss or verified hit can yield one original held relation witness.
-- An admitted finite unique window projects complete Binary keys and winning fact holders with clipped valid intervals.
-- A held decision mask applies to fact assertions before key selection, and a masked or historical window issues no commit witness.
-- An unmasked present window may issue a relation-frontier witness after admitting the complete bounded result.
-- The ordered resolver validates the final staged key set with typed holders and commits occupancy and rows together.
-- Key conflicts expose neither the occupied key nor its holder.
-- A held range requires an exact published covering generation for the captured frontier and serves one page directly from that path.
-- DML evaluates CURRENT_TIMESTAMP at the recorded receiver decision reading when resolving the ordered transaction.
-- An ordinary SQL transaction can assert that this time precedes an exclusive bound.
-- Subsequent SQL mutations can use the same time plus intervals in values, encoded keys and validity endpoints.
-- An idle native SQL session can set bijection.at to a fixed instant.
-- Reads use that instant for the query clock and default valid time.
-- The session refuses writes until the setting is cleared.
-- A native SQL insert can put many documents in one operation with distinct valid-time bounds.
-- The documents retain their input order, including overlapping assertions for the same identity.
-- A null start defaults to the receiver decision reading and a null end defaults to the open end.
-- EPOCH_NANOS_BE(CURRENT_TIMESTAMP) encodes that instant as eight unsigned big-endian bytes without rounding.
--
-- == Frontend parity (the point of this spec) ==
--
-- All three frontends — pgwire, ADBC and Flight SQL — drive one shared Connection, and the model reaches each through the entry points that frontend owns.
--
--   1. pgwire: BEGIN / START TRANSACTION [modes], BEGIN [READ ONLY | READ WRITE] WITH (...),
--      COMMIT [SYNC | ASYNC] and ROLLBACK sent as SQL are pgwire's alone.
--      pgwire parses them itself.
--      BEGIN evaluates and validates its characteristics, then publishes the open transaction with those characteristics atomically.
--      COMMIT and ROLLBACK call the connection's commit and rollback.
--      COPY (query) TO STDOUT WITH (FORMAT 'arrow-stream') uses the connection's read basis.
--      The connection streams Arrow IPC in CopyData frames and ends it before the CopyDone and command completion frames.
--      A COPY query can offer the history label dictionary through a versioned comment immediately inside its opening parenthesis.
--      An accepting owner encodes the selected Utf8 label column as a bounded dictionary of durable IDs and exact stored spellings at the same read basis.
--      The ordinary Utf8 projection remains valid when the owner ignores the offer.
--      Dictionary frames and their rows share each batch's original delivery grant.
--      COPY FROM STDIN with an Arrow format submits Arrow document batches.
--      Without an explicit transaction, each input batch commits separately.
--      A pgwire connection through an RTDB Multi gateway keeps its connection await token after an idle backend replacement.
--      An implicit linearizable basis names the exact, held, applied certified cut selected during the invocation by active-loglet quorum probes.
--      The selected decision must have quorum installation before exposure, including when the receiver has to complete installation.
--      The cut covers all prior quorum-installed decisions and completed fresh reads of the same history.
--      A silent writer and uncertified staged suffix add no dependency when a quorum, hold and selected applied cut are available.
--      An enrolled ordered reader can protect replay and apply the barrier while the writer grant is vacant.
--      Ordinary Flight release consumes durable, authenticated range evidence at a protected applied cut of the same history.
--      A replay-safe unpinned read may retry within the same lineage before output, with its causal floor and audience intact.
--      A read transaction retains its captured basis across later statements.
--      Each explicit snapshot token identifies the basis the caller selected.
--      The gateway restores the mutable settings and token before replaying prepared statements or resuming application work.
--      The mutable settings include the timezone, session parameters and default transaction access mode.
--      DISCARD ALL restores the original startup settings.
--      SQL PREPARE keeps the search path and timezone at definition across a gateway reconnect.
--   2. ADBC and Flight SQL: a caller handing the connection SQL text has BEGIN, START TRANSACTION, COMMIT and ROLLBACK rejected with xtdb.adbc/tx-control-as-sql, which names the transaction API.
--      These callers open and end transactions through their API: ADBC autocommit / commit / rollback, Flight SQL BeginTransaction / EndTransaction, and Xtdb.Connection.begin / commitSync / commitAsync / rollbackTx.
--      They set characteristics with SET TRANSACTION, which is accepted as SQL, or with Xtdb.Connection.setTransactionReadOnly / setTransactionReadWrite / setTransactionTimeZone.
--
--      SET TRANSACTION modes is accepted as SQL from every frontend, classified by the SqlParser, so access mode, read basis, commit-opts and the transaction's time zone are all expressible there.
--
-- SHOW rtdb_source_barrier waits for the current database to apply the durable source tip it samples during the command.
-- It is an idle-session operation and does not open or pin a transaction.
-- A subsequent read on that connection observes at least that applied cut, including submissions from other sessions.
-- Databases with external transaction sources and nodes without indexing cannot serve this barrier.
-- SHOW rtdb_hosted_source_barrier is the hosted variant and returns the confirmed deployment identity.
-- It requires a current holder with linearizable reads, a recovered directory checkpoint, and a fresh quorum confirmation.
-- The ordinary hosted Flight source confirmation uses the same barrier before checking the original positions at one admitted catalog cut.
-- The exact source position and system revision identify one transaction row in that catalog.
-- A returned decision clock comes from the committed row with both of those coordinates.
-- The authenticated placed control owner supplies the deployment identity and up to four exact transaction ID and system-time pairs.
-- The Flight reply carries the sampled applied position as Arrow columns only when the cut proves every supplied position.
--
-- An ordinary authenticated Flight session may publish a cell catalog batch when the owner advertises PublishCatalog.v1.
-- The wire batch carries binary keys, dictionary-encoded operations and labels, unit lists and the offsets of the eight-byte ordered value stamp slots.
-- The owner lowers those values to the installed catalog relation layout and submits the caller's ordered guards, rows and optional attempt marker in one SQL transaction under the caller's database authority.
-- A synchronous reply carries the exact transaction ID, ordered system timestamp, session await token and ordered outcome, including the first reached assertion refusal.
-- An interrupted response leaves the transaction outcome to the attempt marker and durable source observation.
-- An ordinary authenticated Flight session may read a bound catalog projection through ScanCatalog.v1 under the SQL snapshot issued to the same principal and database.
-- The held snapshot supplies the original read cut and clock, and each streamed Arrow result frame requires its delivery grant.
-- The packed hosted decision accepts related Arrow runs in a shared stream and restores their scheduled order before the existing decision, control, and source admission.
--
-- == Boundaries ==
--
-- Below: db.allium's submitTx / executeTx. A Writing commit drains the buffer and
--        crosses this boundary (submit when async, execute-and-await when sync). The
--        async/sync choice is the commit's own COMMIT SYNC / COMMIT ASYNC if it states one,
--        else the transaction's begin-time async flag.
-- Above: the Frontend (pgwire / ADBC / Flight SQL client) that decodes the wire protocol and feeds SQL strings and transaction API calls into the Connection.
-- A trusted frontend may supply a private replay permit for the synchronous commit of exactly one buffered write operation.
-- The permit names the command's effective key and exclusive submission cutoff.
-- The initial attempt and every retry of that command carry the same permit.
-- The guarded source record prevents a legacy resolver from applying the user's operations.
-- A conflicting key in the transaction metadata is rejected before append.
-- A retry that reaches marker lookup and finds the original outcome returns the original committed transaction key.

---- Given

given {
    connection: Connection
}

---- External Entities

external entity Frontend {
    -- The protocol frontend driving the connection: pgwire, or an ADBC / Flight SQL client.
    -- It decodes the wire protocol and feeds SQL strings (and, for ADBC and Flight SQL, the transaction API calls) into the Connection.
    -- This is the party on the far side.
    protocol: pgwire | adbc | flight_sql
}

external entity Database {
    -- The db.allium submit/execute boundary. submitTx is fire-and-forget; executeTx
    -- submits then awaits the result. A Writing commit drains the buffer across this
    -- boundary — submitting when async, executing-and-awaiting when sync, where the choice is
    -- the commit's own COMMIT SYNC / COMMIT ASYNC if stated, else the tx's begin-time async
    -- flag. See: dev/doc/db.allium, database/Database.kt
}

external entity SqlParser {
    -- The SQL classifier (xtdb.query.SqlParser → ParsedStatement).
    -- Recognises tx-control statements (Begin / Commit / Rollback) and statement kinds (Query vs Dml) without planning.
    -- Validates every transaction option occurrence and retains the last occurrence of each option for resolution.
    -- ASYNC accepts a literal or DEFAULT.
    -- Ordinary SQL in a Simple Query message has its full grammar checked before a statement can affect the session.
    -- Native pgwire reports lexical errors in that message before grammar errors.
    -- Grammar validation reports the first invalid statement in source order.
    -- An initial Simple Query statement error distinguishes a mismatch from an unwanted token and carries the received SQL in its structured detail.
    -- Each parsed statement resolves its operands when the connection reaches it.
    -- Native Extended Parse lexes the full input before classifying the statement.
    -- If classification cannot reserve memory, existing prepared statements remain registered.
    -- Bind reserves argument slots before publishing a portal.
    -- Extended Parse validates the grammar of every nested PREPARE body before installing the prepared statement.
    -- Executing a PREPARE prepares its immediate child in the current session context.
    -- Native pgwire reports locations in structured errors from a reached SQL PREPARE child as byte positions in the normalized Simple Query message that supplied it.
    -- pgwire acts on the tx-control statements it classifies; the other frontends reject them.
    -- See: core/src/main/kotlin/xtdb/query/SqlParser.kt
}

external entity SqlPlanner {
    -- The connection's expression evaluator (xtdb.query.SqlPlanner). Sibling of the
    -- SqlParser: where the parser CLASSIFIES a statement, the planner EVALUATES a
    -- standalone transaction-option expression to its runtime value — it plans the
    -- expression and substitutes positional params, turning e.g. SYSTEM_TIME's
    -- `TIMESTAMP '…'` into a concrete instant. Injected (an SPI), shared with pgwire.
    -- See: core/src/main/kotlin/xtdb/query/SqlPlanner.kt
}

external entity User {
    -- The session identity, established once at authentication: by the frontend's handshake, or by an in-process caller connecting with credentials.
    -- Stamped onto every transaction this connection submits.
    -- Not a per-transaction option.
    -- An in-process connection opened without credentials has none.
}

---- Value Types

-- Host-platform types carried opaquely at this level. (Instants map to the Allium
-- primitive Timestamp; ZoneId and Bytes have no primitive equivalent, so they are
-- structureless value types — opaque by-value carriers.)
value ZoneId {
    -- java.time.ZoneId — a connection / transaction timezone.
}

value Bytes {
    -- Opaque serialized bytes (e.g. per-transaction user metadata).
}

value TxBasisToken {
    -- An opaque await-token: the read basis carried between connection and Database so
    -- a connection's reads observe (at least) its own prior writes. Each write advances
    -- it; each read threads it through. See: database/encodeTxBasisToken.
}

value ExactSnapshotToken {
    -- An opaque authenticated reference to a captured applied cut, including empty partitions, original history, catalog and ancestry.
    -- The reference binds interpretation and authorization scope, temporal selectors, the exact query clock and timezone.
    -- Use requires current authorization, custody and an available hold.
}

value ReadBasis {
    -- The first query of a transaction pins an exact applied cut and its evaluation context.
    -- A fresh admission satisfies the effective causal prerequisite, waits for apply and obtains the residence hold.
    -- The system-time cap filters events only inside that cut.
    -- An exact re-admission checks the issued cut and interpretation before opening storage.
    -- A conflicting selector or a causal requirement beyond that cut refuses.
    snapshot_token: ExactSnapshotToken
    snapshot_time: Timestamp?
    clock_time: Timestamp
}

value Unspecified {
    -- Characteristics of a BEGIN that declared no access mode. Carries nothing.
}

value ReadOnly {
    -- Characteristics declared by BEGIN or SET TRANSACTION READ ONLY, evaluated then and held until the first query pins the read basis.
    -- Each absent option takes its default at the pin.
    -- A snapshot token selects its original cap, clock and timezone. Fresh admission can specify a cap and evaluation clock.
    --   await_token — overrides the connection's await_token for this transaction's pin.
    --                 Validated (decoded) when declared; the await itself happens at the pin.
    snapshot_token: ExactSnapshotToken?
    snapshot_time: Timestamp?
    clock_time: Timestamp?
    await_token: TxBasisToken?
}

value CommitOpts {
    -- Write-side options, grammar-scoped to READ WRITE (readWriteTxOption in the SQL
    -- grammar). Their very presence implies the transaction is Writing.
    -- The transaction's effective timezone is NOT a commit-opt: it is set by BEGIN or SET TRANSACTION as the tx's tx_default_tz (see Transaction), not deferred to COMMIT.
    -- Automatic history time is the maximum of the preceding value and the append observation,
    -- or the append observation at genesis. Owned publications require a distinct history coordinate.
    --   user_metadata — per-transaction metadata.
    --                   New source-log and external submissions project user metadata in xt.txs until the installed receipt clock reaches one hour after admission.
    --                   A reusable SQL retry key has separate stored receipt identities for separate hour buckets.
    --                   Lookup covers the current and previous buckets and checks the exclusive expiry.
    --                   A typed metadata association names one ordinary record or an exact record in the submitting owner's Root.
    --                   Its payload is retained only when the transaction commits a put for that record without erasing it.
    --                   An ordered record erase erases its associated metadata in the same commit.
    --                   The installed catalog determines retention even when a query selects an earlier system time.
    --                   A settled retry keeps its original causal coordinate after that metadata expires.
    --                   An idempotency_key field names an exact transaction retry identity scoped to the registered receiver, database and authenticated principal.
    --                   The hosted named-await carrier binds the Original task owner, request address and descriptor to the ordered command.
    --                   A hosted function completion orders its fate with the application writes and substitutes the exact refusal fate when the resolver chooses the constraint refusal.
    --                   A same-body retry within the receipt window returns the original outcome and a changed body aborts with rtdb/idempotency-conflict.
    --                   Application owners register business intents and compare any labelled commands at their own decision boundaries.
    --                   Within one installed history, receipt admission and expiry use the original certified decision reading.
    --                   The native rtdb_retry_v1 field takes a document with a lowercase 32-hex-digit nonce and an integer not_after_micros.
    --                   Its principal-scoped identity includes that exclusive UTC-microsecond deadline, which is within one hour of the admission clock.
    --                   A same-body retry before expiry returns the first terminal outcome, including an abort.
    --                   A different body returns rtdb/idempotency-conflict, and any retry at or after expiry returns rtdb/idempotency-expired.
    --                   The engine uses an independently advanced certified clock with a durable monotone floor across restarts.
    --                   A new operation uses a fresh identity, and rtdb_retry_v1 is mutually exclusive with idempotency_key.
    --                   PgWire exposes the native protocol with SHOW rtdb_bounded_retry = 1, checked on each new connection before a bounded submission.
    --                   For an owned publication, the deciding history orders the guarded decision and slot witness with the effects.
    --                   Repair retains the original slot and position and applies current custody masks.
    --                   A successor installs authenticated exclusion and transfer evidence before admitting inherited work.
    --                   SQL retry metadata expiry ends readback. Owned execution exclusion survives through its separate fence.
    --   async         — true: commit submits without awaiting; false: execute-and-await.
    --                    The begin-time default; a COMMIT SYNC / COMMIT ASYNC (see CommitMode)
    --                    overrides it for that one commit.
    user_metadata: Bytes?
    async: Boolean
}

value OptionSetting {
    -- One option as a SET TRANSACTION or BEGIN names it: the value its expression evaluates to, or null for DEFAULT, which clears the option.
    -- An option the statement doesn't name has no OptionSetting at all.
    value: Any?
}

value ReadOnlySettings {
    -- The read-only options a READ ONLY statement names; each one it doesn't name is null.
    snapshot_token: OptionSetting?
    snapshot_time: OptionSetting?
    clock_time: OptionSetting?
    await_token: OptionSetting?
}

value ReadWriteSettings {
    -- The read-write options a READ WRITE statement names; each one it doesn't name is null.
    user_metadata: OptionSetting?
    async: OptionSetting?
}

value TxOp {
    -- A single buffered operation (a SQL DML statement plus its column-major args, or a
    -- doc put). Opaque at this level. See: tx/TxOp.kt
}

---- Enumerations

enum StatementKind {
    -- How the SqlParser classifies an executable statement for access-mode resolution.
    -- A query pins a pending tx's read basis, making it Reading; a DML statement makes
    -- a Pending(Unspecified) tx Writing. (DDL that submits as tx-ops — CREATE TABLE, GRANT — classifies as dml.)
    -- A CREATE TABLE can contribute nullable native column types to the ordered catalog even with no row writes.
    query | dml
}

enum BeginAccessMode {
    -- The access mode carried explicitly on a BEGIN or SET TRANSACTION. `read_only` /
    -- `read_write` fix the mode; `none` states no access mode (an isolation level or a time zone only), which leaves the mode as it was.
    -- This is distinct from inferring the mode from non-null read options or commit-opts: it lets `READ ONLY` with no options still make the transaction Pending(ReadOnly).
    read_only | read_write | none
}

enum SessionAccessMode {
    -- The connection's session-level default access mode, set by SET SESSION CHARACTERISTICS
    -- AS TRANSACTION READ ONLY / READ WRITE. When set, a bare BEGIN (access_mode = none) takes
    -- this default; absent (null), a bare BEGIN opens Pending(Unspecified).
    read_only | read_write
}

enum CommitMode {
    -- A commit-time submit-vs-execute override carried on a SQL COMMIT [SYNC | ASYNC].
    -- `sync` forces execute-and-await, `async` forces fire-and-forget submit. Passed as an
    -- optional CommitTransaction parameter (null = no commit-time override, defer to the
    -- transaction's begin-time CommitOpts.async flag). Distinct from that begin-time flag: it
    -- lets a single COMMIT override the mode fixed by the transaction's ASYNC option.
    sync | async
}

enum EndTransactionAction {
    -- The outcome Flight SQL's EndTransaction asks for.
    commit | rollback
}

enum HandleFailure {
    -- Why a call naming a Flight SQL transaction handle reaches no transaction.
    -- Both fail the call NOT_FOUND; the description says which.
    expired | unknown
}

---- Entities and Variants

entity Connection {
    -- A session-scoped handle onto the node, shared by both protocol frontends.
    -- See: core/src/main/kotlin/xtdb/api/Xtdb.kt (Xtdb.Connection)
    user: User?                      -- session identity, set once at authentication; null for an in-process connection opened without credentials
    auto_commit: Boolean
    -- In-process connections inherit the node's configured zone, which defaults to the fixed UTC offset Z.
    -- PostgreSQL startup uses the configured zone when present and defaults to the UTC region.
    default_tz: ZoneId
    opened_tz: ZoneId                -- the default_tz the connection was constructed with
    await_token: TxBasisToken?       -- advanced by every write, threaded through every read
    default_access_mode: SessionAccessMode?  -- set by SET SESSION CHARACTERISTICS; null leaves a bare BEGIN unspecified

    -- At most one open transaction. Singular relationship: the value is the open
    -- Transaction, or null when none is open.
    tx: Transaction with connection = this

    invariant AtMostOneOpenTransaction {
        -- Enforced structurally by the singular relationship: a connection has at most
        -- one open Transaction at a time. BEGIN while tx != null is an error
        -- (see BeginWhileOpenRejected).
        true
    }
}

entity Transaction {
    -- One open transaction on a connection. It is in one of three phases: Pending (BEGIN's
    -- characteristics declared, nothing pinned), Reading (read basis pinned) or Writing
    -- (buffering DML). `failed` records an abort: once set, the
    -- transaction rejects all further statements until rolled back.
    -- See: Xtdb.Connection.Transaction
    connection: Connection
    mode: Pending | Reading | Writing
    failed: Boolean
    tx_default_tz: ZoneId            -- the tz this tx's DML and reads anchor in (the TIMEZONE BEGIN or SET TRANSACTION set, else the connection default at BEGIN)
    session_default_tz: ZoneId       -- the connection default_tz to write back to the connection on COMMIT
}

variant Pending : Transaction {
    -- Opened by BEGIN with no access mode (Unspecified) or BEGIN READ ONLY (ReadOnly). No read
    -- basis is pinned yet. The first query pins it, making the tx Reading; DML makes an
    -- Unspecified tx Writing and is rejected in a ReadOnly one (see FirstQueryPinsReadBasis /
    -- FirstStatementResolvesWriting / DmlInReadOnlyRejected).
    characteristics: Unspecified | ReadOnly
}

variant Reading : Transaction {
    -- A read-only transaction. Carries the read basis its reads observe, and the await_token
    -- override its pin used, if any. XTDB read-only transactions are READ-only in the strict
    -- sense: DML is rejected (see DmlInReadOnlyRejected).
    read_basis: ReadBasis
    await_token: TxBasisToken?
}

variant Writing : Transaction {
    -- A write-only transaction. Buffers DML; queries are rejected (see QueryInWritingRejected).
    -- Carries the commit-opts that govern how the buffer is submitted at COMMIT.
    buffer: DmlBuffer with transaction = this
    commit_opts: CommitOpts
}

entity DmlBuffer {
    -- Accumulates buffered ops for a Writing transaction. Consecutive same-SQL ops
    -- coalesce: their args are appended column-major into the last op, so a prepared
    -- INSERT executed in a loop becomes one multi-row op. Drained at COMMIT.
    -- See: Xtdb.Connection.DmlBuffer
    transaction: Transaction
    ops: List<TxOp>
}

entity FlightSqlTransaction {
    -- The handle Flight SQL's BeginTransaction returns, naming a transaction on a connection of its own.
    -- An authorised call presenting the handle reaches that transaction from another Flight SQL session in the same scope.
    -- Placed Flight SQL handles are further scoped to the local attachment.
    -- Closing or expiring a session does not end it: only EndTransaction or the idle timeout does.
    connection: Connection
    status: open | expired
    last_named_at: Timestamp
    expired_at: Timestamp?
}

---- Config

config {
    -- Flight SQL's transaction_idle_timeout: how long an open handle may go unnamed before its transaction is rolled back.
    transaction_idle_timeout: Duration = 30.minutes
}

---- Rules: Beginning a Transaction

-- An explicit transaction opens regardless of auto_commit; the flag is left untouched and the explicit transaction coexists with it.
-- pgwire opens one with SQL BEGIN; ADBC and Flight SQL open one through their transaction API (see Rules: Tx-Control SQL Outside pgwire).
--
-- pgwire's BEGIN / START TRANSACTION take the same list of modes as SET TRANSACTION.
-- BEGIN also takes `READ ONLY WITH (...)` / `READ WRITE WITH (...)`, whose WITH list holds that access mode's options and TIMEZONE.
-- BEGIN evaluates its option expressions on the initiating call and validates their values before publishing the transaction.
-- The published transaction combines the session defaults with BEGIN's characteristics using the SET TRANSACTION merge rules.
-- Rejection or cancellation during evaluation leaves the connection without an open transaction.
--
-- No BEGIN pins a read basis: that happens at the transaction's first query. The phase a
-- BEGIN opens depends on the session default access mode:
--   READ ONLY default  → Pending(ReadOnly with no options)
--   READ WRITE default → Writing, empty buffer (no basis — writes don't read)
--   no default         → Pending(Unspecified), resolved by the first statement

rule BeginOpensTransaction {
    -- The event carries successfully evaluated and validated characteristics.
    -- Transaction creation and the characteristic merge form one publication.
    -- Session defaults supply the phase and timezone before BEGIN's supplied characteristics are merged.
    when: BeginTransaction(frontend, connection, access_mode, read_only_opts, commit_opts, tz_override)
    requires: connection.tx = null

    ensures:
        if connection.default_access_mode = read_only:
            Pending.created(
                connection: connection,
                failed: false,
                tx_default_tz: connection.default_tz,
                session_default_tz: connection.default_tz,
                characteristics: ReadOnly(
                    snapshot_token: null,
                    snapshot_time: null,
                    clock_time: null,
                    await_token: null
                )
            )
        else if connection.default_access_mode = read_write:
            let buffer = DmlBuffer.created(ops: {})
            Writing.created(
                connection: connection,
                failed: false,
                tx_default_tz: connection.default_tz,
                session_default_tz: connection.default_tz,
                buffer: buffer,
                commit_opts: CommitOpts(
                user_metadata: null,
                    async: false
                )
            )
        else:
            Pending.created(
                connection: connection,
                failed: false,
                tx_default_tz: connection.default_tz,
                session_default_tz: connection.default_tz,
                characteristics: Unspecified
            )
        SetTransaction(
            frontend: frontend,
            connection: connection,
            access_mode: access_mode,
            read_only_opts: read_only_opts,
            commit_opts: commit_opts,
            tz_override: tz_override
        )
}

rule BeginRejectedCharacteristicsLeaveConnectionIdle {
    when: BeginCharacteristicsRejected(frontend, connection, error_code)
    requires: connection.tx = null

    ensures:
        not exists connection.tx
        BeginRejected(error_code: error_code)
}

rule BeginWhileOpenRejected {
    -- At most one open transaction per connection: BEGIN while one is open is an error.
    when: BeginTransaction(frontend, connection, access_mode, read_only_opts, commit_opts, tz_override)
    requires: connection.tx != null

    ensures:
        BeginRejected(error_code: `xtdb/tx-already-open`)
}

---- Rules: Setting Transaction Characteristics

-- SET TRANSACTION takes a comma-separated list of transaction modes, the access mode anywhere in it:
--   READ ONLY | READ WRITE — the access mode
--   SNAPSHOT_TOKEN | SNAPSHOT_TIME | CLOCK_TIME | AWAIT_TOKEN = ... — read-only options
--   SYSTEM_TIME | METADATA | ASYNC = ... — read-write options
--   TIMEZONE = ... — the transaction's time zone
--   ISOLATION LEVEL ... — accepted, with no effect: XTDB is always serializable
-- A read-only or read-write option needs its access mode in the same statement, or the statement doesn't parse; TIMEZONE and ISOLATION LEVEL need none.
-- The event carries the access mode (see BeginAccessMode), the options of that mode the statement names (read_only_opts as ReadOnlySettings, commit_opts as ReadWriteSettings), and the time zone if it names one (tz_override, an OptionSetting).
-- Each option's value is an expression, evaluated through the SqlPlanner with the statement's bound args, or DEFAULT, which clears it.
-- Instants without a zone are read in the transaction's time zone as this same statement resolves it.
--
-- A SET TRANSACTION merges into the characteristics set before it.
-- Within the access mode the transaction already has, it sets only the options it names and keeps the rest; switching access mode drops the other mode's options.
-- The time zone belongs to the transaction, not to its access mode: it is kept across a switch of access mode and across a SET TRANSACTION that doesn't name it, and TIMEZONE = DEFAULT resets it to session_default_tz.
-- It sets the tx's tx_default_tz only, so it reverts at COMMIT and ROLLBACK as BEGIN's does.
--
-- A SET TRANSACTION naming an access mode or a time zone is accepted while the transaction is Pending, or Writing with an empty buffer.
-- Once a query has pinned the read basis (Reading), or a Writing tx has buffered ops, it is rejected.
-- One naming neither is accepted in any state and changes nothing.
--
-- Xtdb.Connection.setTransactionReadOnly / setTransactionReadWrite / setTransactionTimeZone are the same operation without SQL.
-- Each argument sets its option, null meaning not given, so they cannot clear one.

rule SetTransactionReadOnly {
    -- READ ONLY makes the phase Pending(ReadOnly), with the options evaluated now and held until the first query pins the basis they override (see FirstQueryPinsReadBasis).
    -- A Pending(ReadOnly) tx keeps the options this statement doesn't name; any other phase starts with none.
    -- A malformed AWAIT_TOKEN is rejected here; the await itself happens at the pin.
    when: SetTransaction(frontend, connection, access_mode, read_only_opts, commit_opts, tz_override)
    requires: connection.tx != null
    requires: connection.tx.mode = Pending or (connection.tx.mode = Writing and connection.tx.buffer.ops.count = 0)
    requires: access_mode = read_only

    ensures:
        let prior =
            if connection.tx.mode = Pending and connection.tx.characteristics = ReadOnly: connection.tx.characteristics
            else: null
        connection.tx.mode = Pending
        connection.tx.characteristics = ReadOnly(
            snapshot_token:
                if read_only_opts?.snapshot_token != null: read_only_opts.snapshot_token.value
                else: prior?.snapshot_token,
            snapshot_time:
                if read_only_opts?.snapshot_time != null: read_only_opts.snapshot_time.value
                else: prior?.snapshot_time,
            clock_time:
                if read_only_opts?.clock_time != null: read_only_opts.clock_time.value
                else: prior?.clock_time,
            await_token:
                if read_only_opts?.await_token != null: read_only_opts.await_token.value
                else: prior?.await_token
        )
        connection.tx.tx_default_tz =
            if tz_override != null: tz_override.value ?? connection.tx.session_default_tz
            else: connection.tx.tx_default_tz
}

rule SetTransactionReadWrite {
    -- READ WRITE makes the phase Writing with an empty buffer: a Writing tx carries no read basis, as writes don't read.
    -- A Writing tx keeps the commit-opts this statement doesn't name; any other phase starts with the defaults (no metadata, synchronous).
    -- ASYNC = DEFAULT makes the commit synchronous.
    when: SetTransaction(frontend, connection, access_mode, read_only_opts, commit_opts, tz_override)
    requires: connection.tx != null
    requires: connection.tx.mode = Pending or (connection.tx.mode = Writing and connection.tx.buffer.ops.count = 0)
    requires: access_mode = read_write

    ensures:
        let prior =
            if connection.tx.mode = Writing: connection.tx.commit_opts
            else: null
        let buffer = DmlBuffer.created(ops: {})
        connection.tx.mode = Writing
        connection.tx.buffer = buffer
        connection.tx.commit_opts = CommitOpts(
            user_metadata:
                if commit_opts?.user_metadata != null: commit_opts.user_metadata.value
                else: prior?.user_metadata,
            async:
                if commit_opts?.async != null: commit_opts.async.value ?? false
                else: prior?.async ?? false
        )
        connection.tx.tx_default_tz =
            if tz_override != null: tz_override.value ?? connection.tx.session_default_tz
            else: connection.tx.tx_default_tz
}

rule SetTransactionTimeZoneOnly {
    -- A SET TRANSACTION naming a time zone but no access mode sets the tx's tx_default_tz and leaves the phase and its options as they are.
    when: SetTransaction(frontend, connection, access_mode, read_only_opts, commit_opts, tz_override)
    requires: connection.tx != null
    requires: connection.tx.mode = Pending or (connection.tx.mode = Writing and connection.tx.buffer.ops.count = 0)
    requires: access_mode = none
    requires: tz_override != null

    ensures:
        connection.tx.tx_default_tz = tz_override.value ?? connection.tx.session_default_tz
}

rule SetTransactionAfterFirstStatementRejected {
    -- A Reading tx has pinned its basis, and a Writing tx with buffered ops has captured
    -- its statements under the current characteristics: neither can be reshaped.
    when: SetTransaction(frontend, connection, access_mode, read_only_opts, commit_opts, tz_override)
    requires: connection.tx != null
    requires: access_mode != none or tz_override != null
    requires: connection.tx.mode = Reading or (connection.tx.mode = Writing and connection.tx.buffer.ops.count > 0)

    ensures:
        StatementRejected(error_code: `xtdb/set-transaction-after-first-statement`)
}

rule SetTransactionOutsideTxRejected {
    -- Under auto-commit there is no transaction to set characteristics on.
    when: SetTransaction(frontend, connection, access_mode, read_only_opts, commit_opts, tz_override)
    requires: connection.tx = null
    requires: access_mode != none or tz_override != null
    requires: connection.auto_commit

    ensures:
        StatementRejected(error_code: `xtdb/set-transaction-outside-tx`)
}

rule SetTransactionOpensTransaction {
    -- Under manual commit with no transaction open, SET TRANSACTION opens a Pending(Unspecified)
    -- transaction first, then applies its characteristics by the rules above.
    when: SetTransaction(frontend, connection, access_mode, read_only_opts, commit_opts, tz_override)
    requires: connection.tx = null
    requires: access_mode != none or tz_override != null
    requires: not connection.auto_commit

    ensures:
        Pending.created(
            connection: connection,
            failed: false,
            tx_default_tz: connection.default_tz,
            session_default_tz: connection.default_tz,
            characteristics: Unspecified
        )
        SetTransaction(
            frontend: frontend,
            connection: connection,
            access_mode: access_mode,
            read_only_opts: read_only_opts,
            commit_opts: commit_opts,
            tz_override: tz_override
        )
}

rule SetTransactionIsolationOnly {
    -- A SET TRANSACTION naming neither an access mode nor a time zone (an isolation level only) is accepted in any state and changes nothing: XTDB is always serializable.
    when: SetTransaction(frontend, connection, access_mode, read_only_opts, commit_opts, tz_override)
    requires: access_mode = none
    requires: tz_override = null

    ensures:
        SetTransactionAccepted(connection: connection)
}

---- Rules: Access-Mode Resolution

-- When BEGIN did not fix the access mode, the first statement resolves it. A query pins the
-- read basis of a Pending transaction, making it Reading; a DML statement makes a
-- Pending(Unspecified) transaction Writing. A Pending(ReadOnly) transaction accepts only
-- queries; a Reading or Writing transaction stays as it is and enforces its direction (see the
-- write-only rules below).

rule FirstQueryPinsReadBasis {
    -- First query in a Pending tx pins the read basis and opens Reading. The pin awaits the
    -- effective causal prerequisite before capture or verifies that the exact basis already covers it.
    -- Admission confirms current scope and obtains a hold before opening storage.
    when: ExecuteStatement(frontend, connection, kind, statement)
    requires: connection.tx != null
    requires: connection.tx.mode = Pending
    requires: kind = query

    ensures:
        let opts = connection.tx.characteristics
        connection.tx.mode = Reading
        connection.tx.read_basis = Database.admitReadBasis(
            exact: opts?.snapshot_token,
            await: opts?.await_token ?? connection.await_token,
            cap: opts?.snapshot_time,
            clock: opts?.clock_time
        )
        connection.tx.await_token = opts?.await_token
}

rule FirstStatementResolvesWriting {
    -- First statement in a Pending(Unspecified) tx is DML → Writing with an empty buffer and
    -- default commit-opts (synchronous); no read basis was pinned.
    -- The DML op itself is then buffered by BufferDml.
    when: ExecuteStatement(frontend, connection, kind, statement)
    requires: connection.tx != null
    requires: connection.tx.mode = Pending
    requires: connection.tx.characteristics = Unspecified
    requires: kind = dml

    ensures:
        let buffer = DmlBuffer.created(ops: {})
        connection.tx.mode = Writing
        connection.tx.buffer = buffer
        connection.tx.commit_opts = CommitOpts(
            user_metadata: null,
            async: false
        )
}

---- Rules: Timezone

-- SET TIME ZONE is a session operator. This spec touches it only for its transaction
-- scoping — the broader SET / SHOW session surface is out of frame (as with SET SESSION
-- CHARACTERISTICS, referenced only as a source of default_access_mode).
-- A TIMEZONE on BEGIN or SET TRANSACTION stays tx-scoped (it sets tx_default_tz, never session_default_tz); a mid-tx SET TIME ZONE writes both, so it persists on COMMIT and reverts on ROLLBACK.

rule SetTimeZoneInTx {
    -- Mid-transaction SET TIME ZONE writes BOTH the tx's tx_default_tz (so this tx's DML and
    -- reads re-anchor) AND its session_default_tz (so the change is written back to the
    -- connection on COMMIT and persists past the tx). The connection's own default_tz is left
    -- untouched while the tx is open — that is what makes the ROLLBACK revert implicit.
    when: SetTimeZone(frontend, connection, zone)
    requires: connection.tx != null
    requires: not (connection.tx.mode = Writing and connection.tx.buffer.ops.count > 0)

    ensures:
        connection.tx.tx_default_tz = zone
        connection.tx.session_default_tz = zone
}

rule SetTimeZoneInWriteTxRejected {
    -- SET TIME ZONE is rejected once a Writing tx has buffered ops: those ops captured
    -- temporal literals under the old zone, so changing it mid-buffer would be inconsistent.
    when: SetTimeZone(frontend, connection, zone)
    requires: connection.tx != null
    requires: connection.tx.mode = Writing
    requires: connection.tx.buffer.ops.count > 0

    ensures:
        StatementRejected(error_code: `xtdb/set-tz-in-write-tx`)
}

rule SetTimeZoneNoTx {
    -- With no open transaction, SET TIME ZONE updates the connection's default_tz directly.
    when: SetTimeZone(frontend, connection, zone)
    requires: connection.tx = null

    ensures:
        connection.default_tz = zone
}

---- Rules: Statement Execution

-- A DML statement auto-executes as its own single-op transaction IFF no explicit
-- transaction is open. Otherwise it buffers into the open transaction — even when
-- auto_commit is on. (An explicit BEGIN coexists with auto_commit; the open tx wins.)
--
-- The auto_commit guards across these rules partition the no-explicit-BEGIN cases cleanly:
--   {auto_commit + no tx}  → AutoCommitDml / AutoCommitQuery (transient per-statement tx)
--   {not auto_commit + no tx} → ManualLazyOpenDml / ManualLazyOpenQuery (lazy-open, below)
--   {tx open}              → BufferDml / QueryInReading / the rejection rules

rule AutoCommitDml {
    -- No explicit transaction open: the DML statement is its own transaction. It submits
    -- through the Database boundary as a single-op execute-and-await. auto_commit must be
    -- on (an explicit BEGIN would have opened a tx, taking the BufferDml path instead).
    when: ExecuteStatement(frontend, connection, kind, statement)
    requires: kind = dml
    requires: connection.tx = null
    requires: connection.auto_commit

    ensures:
        Database.executeTx(
            ops: { statement },
            opts: { user: connection.user, default_tz: connection.default_tz }
        )
        AwaitTokenAdvanced(connection: connection)

    @guidance
        -- A single-statement auto-commit DML carries no buffering; it is the degenerate
        -- case of "open, buffer one op, commit synchronously". executeTx advances the
        -- connection's await_token so subsequent reads on this connection see the write.
}

rule ManualLazyOpenDml {
    -- Manual mode (auto_commit off), no open transaction, statement is DML. Per pgjdbc's
    -- lazy BEGIN — setAutoCommit(false) does not BEGIN immediately; the next statement does
    -- — this opens a Pending(Unspecified) tx and resolves it by the normal rules: DML →
    -- Writing (no basis — writes don't read), with the DML buffered. Open + resolve are one
    -- step here because the resolution rules trigger on a tx that is already open; a freshly
    -- lazily-opened tx must resolve within the same statement.
    when: ExecuteStatement(frontend, connection, kind, statement)
    requires: kind = dml
    requires: connection.tx = null
    requires: not connection.auto_commit

    ensures:
        let buffer = DmlBuffer.created(ops: { statement })
        Writing.created(
            connection: connection,
            failed: false,
            tx_default_tz: connection.default_tz,
            session_default_tz: connection.default_tz,
            buffer: buffer,
            commit_opts: CommitOpts(
                user_metadata: null,
                async: false
            )
        )
}

rule ManualLazyOpenQuery {
    -- Manual mode (auto_commit off), no open transaction, statement is a query. Per pgjdbc's
    -- lazy BEGIN, this opens a Pending(Unspecified) tx and pins its basis by the normal rules
    -- (see FirstQueryPinsReadBasis): the connection's await_token is awaited, then the
    -- latest-completed snapshot and current-time are captured, making the tx Reading. Because the
    -- Reading tx persists, subsequent reads in the manual transaction share this one snapshot —
    -- this is the manual-mode transaction's cross-statement snapshot isolation. Open + pin are
    -- one step (see ManualLazyOpenDml).
    when: ExecuteStatement(frontend, connection, kind, statement)
    requires: kind = query
    requires: connection.tx = null
    requires: not connection.auto_commit

    ensures:
        let opened = Reading.created(
            connection: connection,
            failed: false,
            tx_default_tz: connection.default_tz,
            session_default_tz: connection.default_tz,
            read_basis: Database.admitReadBasis(
                exact: null,
                await: connection.await_token,
                cap: null,
                clock: null
            ),
            await_token: null
        )
        Database.openSnapshot(basis: opened.read_basis)
}

rule BufferDml {
    -- An explicit transaction is open: buffer the DML into it, even when auto_commit is
    -- on. Rejected if the open tx is read-only (see DmlInReadOnlyRejected) or failed
    -- (see FailedTransactionRejectsStatements).
    -- The buffer retains the argument width supplied by the frontend.
    -- Prepared Flight SQL updates retain compatible argument batches in Arrow through source serialization and replay, including updates submitted through GetFlightInfo.
    -- At commit, the database applies the submission and resolution rules for those SQL arguments.
    when: ExecuteStatement(frontend, connection, kind, statement)
    requires: kind = dml
    requires: connection.tx != null
    requires: connection.tx.mode = Writing
    requires: not connection.tx.failed

    ensures:
        connection.tx.buffer.ops.add(statement)

    @guidance
        -- Consecutive same-SQL ops coalesce: rather than appending a distinct op, the
        -- args are appended column-major into the trailing op when its SQL text matches.
        -- This is a buffer-internal representation detail; observably the op is buffered.
}

rule QueryInWritingRejected {
    -- Writing is write-only: a query in a Writing transaction is rejected. The
    -- `not failed` guard yields failed-first precedence: on a failed tx,
    -- FailedTransactionRejectsStatements fires instead (unambiguous).
    when: ExecuteStatement(frontend, connection, kind, statement)
    requires: kind = query
    requires: connection.tx != null
    requires: connection.tx.mode = Writing
    requires: not connection.tx.failed

    ensures:
        StatementRejected(error_code: `xtdb/queries-in-read-write-tx`)
}

rule DmlInReadOnlyRejected {
    -- A transaction is read-only when it is Reading, or Pending with ReadOnly characteristics;
    -- DML in one is rejected. The `not failed` guard yields failed-first precedence: on a failed
    -- tx, FailedTransactionRejectsStatements fires instead (unambiguous).
    when: ExecuteStatement(frontend, connection, kind, statement)
    requires: kind = dml
    requires: connection.tx != null
    requires: connection.tx.mode = Reading or (connection.tx.mode = Pending and connection.tx.characteristics = ReadOnly)
    requires: not connection.tx.failed

    ensures:
        StatementRejected(error_code: `xtdb/read-only-tx`)
}

rule QueryInReading {
    -- A query in a Reading transaction observes the pinned read basis. The basis is
    -- threaded to the Database so reads see exactly that point-in-time.
    when: ExecuteStatement(frontend, connection, kind, statement)
    requires: kind = query
    requires: connection.tx != null
    requires: connection.tx.mode = Reading
    requires: not connection.tx.failed

    ensures:
        Database.openSnapshot(basis: connection.tx.read_basis)
}

rule AutoCommitQuery {
    -- Autocommit, no open transaction: the query transiently promotes to a read-only
    -- transaction. It resolves its basis to the connection's CURRENT basis (await_token +
    -- current-time) at this moment, reads against it, then discards — each autocommit read
    -- is fresh, with no cross-statement snapshot. Behaviourally identical to reading at the
    -- current await_token; framed as a transient per-statement Reading tx so that every
    -- read in the model runs inside a read-only transaction.
    when: ExecuteStatement(frontend, connection, kind, statement)
    requires: kind = query
    requires: connection.tx = null
    requires: connection.auto_commit

    ensures:
        Database.openSnapshot(
            basis: Database.admitReadBasis(
                exact: null,
                await: connection.await_token,
                cap: null,
                clock: null
            )
        )

    @guidance
        -- The transient read-only tx is not materialised as a Transaction entity: it opens,
        -- resolves its basis, reads and discards within the single statement. The contrast
        -- with the manual-mode lazy-open path (ManualLazyOpenQuery) is the lifetime — there
        -- the Reading tx persists and its basis is shared across subsequent reads.
}

rule FailedTransactionRejectsStatements {
    -- A failed transaction rejects queries and DML until rollback, on either resolved mode.
    -- Session-variable SHOW is outside StatementKind: the embedded API permits it in failed transactions, while pgwire retains its command-level failure guard.
    when: ExecuteStatement(frontend, connection, kind, statement)
    requires: connection.tx != null
    requires: connection.tx.failed

    ensures:
        StatementRejected(error_code: `xtdb/tx-failed`)
}

---- Rules: Commit and Rollback

-- pgwire's SQL COMMIT / ROLLBACK act on the explicit transaction regardless of auto_commit.
-- ADBC's programmatic commit() / rollback() are valid only when auto_commit is off.

rule PgwireSingleAutocommitWriteCompletesAfterCommit {
    when: SimpleQueryExecuted(frontend, connection, statement)

    requires: frontend.protocol = pgwire
    requires: SingleImplicitDml(statement, connection)

    ensures:
        let outcome = CommitOutcome(connection)
        if outcome.committed:
            CommandComplete(frontend, statement)
        else:
            StatementRejected(error_code: outcome.error_code)
}

rule TxControlAsSqlRejected {
    -- BEGIN, START TRANSACTION, COMMIT and ROLLBACK reach the connection as SQL text from ADBC and Flight SQL callers; the error names the transaction API those callers use.
    when: TxControlSubmittedAsSql(frontend, connection, statement)
    requires: frontend.protocol != pgwire

    ensures:
        StatementRejected(error_code: `xtdb.adbc/tx-control-as-sql`)
}

rule CommitWriting {
    -- Commit of a Writing transaction drains the buffer and crosses the Database
    -- boundary EVEN IF the buffer is empty: async commit submits (fire-and-forget),
    -- sync commit executes-and-awaits. The submit anchors in the tx's tx_default_tz. COMMIT
    -- also writes the tx's session_default_tz back to the connection, so a mid-tx SET TIME
    -- ZONE persists past the tx while a BEGIN or SET TRANSACTION TIMEZONE (which never touches session_default_tz) does not.
    --
    -- The effective async choice: a commit-time COMMIT SYNC / COMMIT ASYNC (commit_mode) wins;
    -- otherwise the transaction's begin-time CommitOpts.async flag decides. Only the SOURCE of
    -- the submit-vs-execute decision gains the override — the split itself is unchanged.
    when: CommitTransaction(frontend, connection, commit_mode)
    requires: connection.tx != null
    requires: connection.tx.mode = Writing
    requires: not connection.tx.failed

    ensures:
        let ops = connection.tx.buffer.ops
        let opts = {
            user: connection.user,
            default_tz: connection.tx.tx_default_tz,
            user_metadata: connection.tx.commit_opts.user_metadata
        }

        let async =
            if commit_mode = async: true
            else if commit_mode = sync: false
            else: connection.tx.commit_opts.async

        connection.default_tz = connection.tx.session_default_tz

        if async:
            Database.submitTx(ops: ops, opts: opts)
        else:
            Database.executeTx(ops: ops, opts: opts)

        AwaitTokenAdvanced(connection: connection)
        not exists connection.tx

    @guidance
        -- "even if empty" is deliberate: an explicit COMMIT of a Writing tx that
        -- buffered nothing still submits an empty transaction. This differs from the
        -- Reading / Pending no-op below.
}

-- In a placed deployment, COMMIT ASYNC sets the connection's await token to an opaque attempt receipt.
-- The holder's bounded memory intake contains provisional offers.
-- A placed submit acknowledges only after observing the exact ordered reservation or its terminal witness.
-- Covered effectful admission reserves the original slot and funds its terminal witness under the exact request binding.
-- Exact retry redeems the original slot, and a retired issuance namespace excludes recreation.
-- The effect or refusal and the receiver's exact terminal witness occupy one ordered record.
-- Closure competes in that order and excludes all late copies at that receiver if it wins.
-- An internal append receipt requires durable decision installation on a quorum.
-- COMMIT SYNC returns an applied replica prefix containing the exact quorum-installed witness.
-- Strict guarded admission counts only original durable votes from its recorded roster with an admissible upper sample strictly before cutoff.
-- The same decision installation gates visibility.
-- A fenced attempt with missing evidence remains unknown unless durable evidence excludes an original timely quorum.
-- A missing witness or exclusive receipt expiry leaves unresolved obligations charged and does not settle the effect.
-- The Original terminal transfer receipt binds the certified replica interval and the exact unmatched receiver reservation.
-- The destination verifies the census while held and retains the original slot through publication.
-- The successor orders the inherited slot's fence before opening fresh receiver admission.
-- An applied prefix remains a causal basis after receipt expiry.
-- An ordered erasure can collect settled evidence after its retention, holds and export obligations end.
-- Before pinning an implicit read basis, redeem the attempt and wait for its exact installed applied prefix.
-- A supplied exact snapshot token fixes the original applied cut and evaluation context.
-- A frozen placed attachment confirms await tokens from its own admitted checkpoint before pinning the read basis.

rule CommitNoOp {
    -- Commit of a Reading or still-Pending transaction submits nothing — there is no
    -- buffer to drain. It still writes the tx's session_default_tz back to the connection
    -- (COMMIT writes the session tz back regardless of mode, before the Writing / no-op
    -- split), so a mid-tx SET TIME ZONE persists on a read-only commit too. The transaction
    -- then closes. commit_mode is irrelevant here — there is nothing to submit, so the
    -- submit-vs-execute override has no effect.
    when: CommitTransaction(frontend, connection, commit_mode)
    requires: connection.tx != null
    requires: connection.tx.mode = Reading or connection.tx.mode = Pending
    requires: not connection.tx.failed

    ensures:
        connection.default_tz = connection.tx.session_default_tz
        not exists connection.tx
}

rule Rollback {
    -- Rollback discards the open transaction (and its buffer, if any), submitting
    -- nothing. Valid even on a failed transaction — this is how a failed tx is cleared.
    -- The connection's default_tz reverts implicitly: it was never mutated while the tx was
    -- open (a mid-tx SET TIME ZONE writes the tx's tx_default_tz / session_default_tz, not
    -- the connection), so dropping the tx discards any mid-transaction SET TIME ZONE.
    -- Postgres semantics — a plain SET in an aborted tx is rolled back; only COMMIT keeps
    -- the new zone (see CommitWriting / CommitNoOp).
    when: RollbackTransaction(frontend, connection)
    requires: connection.tx != null

    ensures:
        not exists connection.tx
}

rule ProgrammaticCommitInAutoCommitRejected {
    -- Programmatic commit() is valid only when auto_commit is off.
    when: ProgrammaticCommit(frontend, connection)
    requires: connection.auto_commit

    ensures:
        CommitRejected(error_code: `xtdb.adbc/commit-in-autocommit`)
}

rule ProgrammaticRollbackInAutoCommitRejected {
    -- Programmatic rollback() is valid only when auto_commit is off.
    when: ProgrammaticRollback(frontend, connection)
    requires: connection.auto_commit

    ensures:
        RollbackRejected(error_code: `xtdb.adbc/rollback-in-autocommit`)
}

rule DisableAutoCommitCommitsOpen {
    -- setAutoCommit(false) when already off is a no-op; turning it ON commits any open
    -- transaction first (so buffered work is not silently stranded), then sets the flag.
    when: SetAutoCommit(frontend, connection, enabled)
    requires: enabled
    requires: not connection.auto_commit

    ensures:
        if connection.tx != null:
            CommitTransaction(frontend: frontend, connection: connection, commit_mode: null)
        connection.auto_commit = true
}

---- Rules: Connection Close

rule CloseConnection {
    -- Closing the connection discards any open transaction WITHOUT submitting. Buffered
    -- but uncommitted work is lost — closing is not an implicit commit.
    when: CloseConnection(frontend, connection)

    ensures:
        if connection.tx != null:
            not exists connection.tx
}

---- Rules: Session Reset

-- pgwire's DISCARD ALL, which a connection pool issues before handing a connection to its next borrower.
-- pgwire then re-applies the startup parameters, and closes every prepared statement and portal.

rule ResetSession {
    -- The session settings return to their construction-time values.
    -- user is kept: it is the session identity, set once at authentication.
    when: ResetSession(frontend, connection)
    requires: connection.tx = null

    ensures:
        connection.default_tz = connection.opened_tz
        connection.await_token = null
        connection.default_access_mode = null
}

rule ResetSessionInTxRejected {
    -- Nothing is reset.
    when: ResetSession(frontend, connection)
    requires: connection.tx != null

    ensures:
        StatementRejected(error_code: `xtdb/discard-in-tx`)
}

---- Rules: Flight SQL Transaction Handles

rule FlightSqlBeginOpensHandle {
    -- BeginTransaction opens a connection for the transaction alone, in manual mode, starting from the caller's await_token and default_tz.
    when: FlightSqlBeginTransaction(frontend, connection)

    ensures:
        let dedicated = Connection.created(
            user: connection.user,
            auto_commit: false,
            default_tz: connection.default_tz,
            await_token: connection.await_token,
            default_access_mode: null
        )
        FlightSqlTransaction.created(connection: dedicated, status: open, last_named_at: now, expired_at: null)
}

rule NamingOpenHandleRenews {
    -- Any call other than EndTransaction that names an open handle runs on the handle's connection, and renews its idle timeout.
    when: NamesTransactionHandle(frontend, handle, end)
    requires: handle.status = open
    requires: end = null

    ensures:
        handle.last_named_at = now
}

rule EndTransactionEndsHandle {
    -- EndTransaction commits or rolls back the handle's transaction, closes its connection, and forgets the handle.
    when: NamesTransactionHandle(frontend, handle, end)
    requires: handle.status = open
    requires: end != null

    ensures:
        if end = commit:
            CommitTransaction(frontend: frontend, connection: handle.connection, commit_mode: null)
        else:
            RollbackTransaction(frontend: frontend, connection: handle.connection)
        CloseConnection(frontend: frontend, connection: handle.connection)
        not exists handle
}

rule IdleHandleExpires {
    -- A handle no call has named for transaction_idle_timeout has its transaction rolled back.
    when: handle: FlightSqlTransaction.last_named_at + config.transaction_idle_timeout <= now
    requires: handle.status = open

    ensures:
        not exists handle.connection.tx
        handle.status = expired
        handle.expired_at = now
}

rule ExpiredHandleForgotten {
    -- An expired handle is reported as expired for a further transaction_idle_timeout, then as unknown.
    when: handle: FlightSqlTransaction.expired_at + config.transaction_idle_timeout <= now
    requires: handle.status = expired

    ensures:
        not exists handle
}

rule NamingExpiredHandleRejected {
    -- The call fails NOT_FOUND, saying the transaction expired.
    when: NamesTransactionHandle(frontend, handle, end)
    requires: handle.status = expired

    ensures:
        HandleRejected(failure: expired)
}

rule NamingUnknownHandleRejected {
    -- A handle that never existed, or whose transaction was ended, fails the call NOT_FOUND as unknown.
    when: UnknownTransactionHandleNamed(frontend)

    ensures:
        HandleRejected(failure: unknown)
}

---- Invariants

invariant WritingCarriesCommitOpts {
    -- Commit-opts exist only on a Writing transaction: their presence implies Writing
    -- (grammar-enforced at BEGIN). The variant structure makes this hold by construction;
    -- stated here as the cross-cutting property.
    true
}

invariant ReadBasisPinnedAtFirstQuery {
    -- The read basis is pinned at the transaction's first query, which takes a Pending tx to
    -- Reading; a Writing tx has none — writes don't read. Structural — stated here as the
    -- cross-cutting property.
    true
}

@invariant ExactSnapshotReAdmission
    -- Re-admission selects the original authenticated cuts and their original winners, subject to live custody.
    -- Later positions cannot enter the cut even when their system time matches or precedes its cap.
    -- Empty partitions remain empty.
    -- A required unavailable dependency refuses without substituting another cut or an older winner.

@invariant ExactQueryClock
    -- The read basis retains the accepted clock instant, including its nanosecond fraction, across subsequent queries and frontend ticket serialization.
    -- Typed local timestamps resolve in the transaction's effective timezone before the clock is pinned.
    -- Clock functions apply their requested result precision to that retained instant.
    -- A microsecond scan boundary or output column converts the retained instant at that boundary.

invariant EffectiveAwaitToken {
    -- The await token SHOW reports for an open tx is the Reading tx's override, or the
    -- Pending(ReadOnly) tx's, else the connection's.
    -- SHOW AWAIT_TOKEN and SHOW LATEST_SUBMITTED_TX return without awaiting this token and preserve the transaction's phase and pinned basis.
    true
}

invariant TxReadOnlyForFrontend {
    -- The frontend's read-only checks treat a tx as read-only when it is Reading, or Pending
    -- with ReadOnly characteristics.
    true
}

---- Actor Declarations

actor FrontendActor {
    -- Any protocol frontend (pgwire, ADBC or Flight SQL).
    -- Any Frontend may drive a connection through the surfaces that provide entry points to every frontend.
    identified_by: Frontend where true
}

actor PgwireFrontend {
    identified_by: Frontend where protocol = pgwire
}

actor TransactionApiFrontend {
    -- An ADBC or Flight SQL client, which opens and ends transactions through its API.
    identified_by: Frontend where protocol != pgwire
}

actor FlightSqlFrontend {
    identified_by: Frontend where protocol = flight_sql
}

---- Surfaces

surface PgwireTxControl {
    -- BEGIN / START TRANSACTION, COMMIT and ROLLBACK as SQL, which pgwire parses itself, and DISCARD ALL.
    facing frontend: PgwireFrontend

    context conn: Connection

    provides:
        BeginTransaction(frontend, conn, access_mode, read_only_opts, commit_opts, tz_override)
            when conn.tx = null
        CommitTransaction(frontend, conn, commit_mode)
            when conn.tx != null
        RollbackTransaction(frontend, conn)
            when conn.tx != null
        ResetSession(frontend, conn)

    @guarantee BeginAtomicPublication
        -- Option evaluation and validation complete before BEGIN publishes an open transaction.
        -- A suspended option evaluation belongs to its initiating command; cancellation ends it and leaves the connection idle.
        -- The published transaction includes every accepted characteristic.

    @guarantee CopyInputRespectsListenerAndSync
        -- A read-only listener refuses COPY input before accepting bytes.
        -- Extended COPY can stream input and ignores Sync until the transfer ends.
        -- The next Sync completes the extended query cycle.
}

surface SqlStatements {
    -- SQL accepted from every frontend; SET TRANSACTION is the SQL route to a transaction's characteristics.
    facing frontend: FrontendActor

    context conn: Connection

    provides:
        SetTransaction(frontend, conn, access_mode, read_only_opts, commit_opts, tz_override)
        ExecuteStatement(frontend, conn, kind, statement)
        SetTimeZone(frontend, conn, zone)

    @guarantee SingleOpenTransaction
        -- At most one transaction is open on a connection at any time.

    @guarantee WriteOnlyWriting
        -- A Writing transaction rejects queries; a read-only transaction rejects DML.

    @guarantee PgwireCancellationDuringSort
        -- The JVM external sorter checks for cancellation while merging spilled runs.
        -- PgWire returns SQLSTATE 57014 when a client cancellation interrupts query execution.

    @guarantee ConnectionScopedIdentity
        -- Every transaction this connection submits is stamped with the connection's
        -- user; it is not a per-transaction option.
}

surface TxControlAsSql {
    -- tx-control statements handed to the connection as SQL text by ADBC and Flight SQL callers.
    facing frontend: TransactionApiFrontend

    context conn: Connection

    provides:
        TxControlSubmittedAsSql(frontend, conn, statement)

    @guarantee TxControlAsSqlNamesTransactionApi
        -- The rejection names the transaction API: ADBC autocommit / commit / rollback, Flight SQL BeginTransaction / EndTransaction, and Xtdb.Connection.begin / commitSync / commitAsync / rollbackTx.
}

surface TransactionApi {
    -- How ADBC and Flight SQL callers open and end transactions, and set their characteristics without SQL: ADBC autocommit / commit / rollback, Flight SQL BeginTransaction / EndTransaction, and Xtdb.Connection.begin / commitSync / commitAsync / rollbackTx / setTransactionReadOnly / setTransactionReadWrite / setTransactionTimeZone.
    facing frontend: TransactionApiFrontend

    context conn: Connection

    provides:
        SetAutoCommit(frontend, conn, enabled)
        ProgrammaticCommit(frontend, conn)
            when not conn.auto_commit
        ProgrammaticRollback(frontend, conn)
            when not conn.auto_commit

    @guarantee ProgrammaticControlNeedsManualMode
        -- Programmatic commit / rollback are valid only when auto_commit is off.
}

surface FlightSqlTransactionHandles {
    -- BeginTransaction, EndTransaction, and every other call that carries a transaction handle.
    facing frontend: FlightSqlFrontend

    context conn: Connection

    provides:
        FlightSqlBeginTransaction(frontend, conn)
        NamesTransactionHandle(frontend, handle, end)
        UnknownTransactionHandleNamed(frontend)

    @guarantee TransactionTimeoutAdvertised
        -- The server advertises transaction_idle_timeout as FLIGHT_SQL_SERVER_TRANSACTION_TIMEOUT, in milliseconds.
}

surface ConnectionLifecycle {
    facing frontend: FrontendActor

    context conn: Connection

    provides:
        CloseConnection(frontend, conn)

    @guarantee AdbcResultCancellation
        -- Native ADBC result release cancels that result's execution while preserving its caller's operation token, connection and sibling results.

    @guarantee CloseDiscardsOpenTx
        -- Closing the connection discards any open transaction without submitting.
        -- Closing is not an implicit commit.
}

---- RTDB v1 ordered transactions

-- Default and Multi mode share one resolver and one fold for rtdb.v1.
-- The serving mode admits validation when its driver can capture a linearizable prefix.
-- The serving mode admits timed writes when its driver can attest the original durable append.
-- A transaction key identifies one request digest within the admitted database incarnation.
-- The resolver returns a stored receipt for an identical request and conflicts on a different request using that key.
-- The writer samples the transaction time at resolution and keeps it strictly increasing with the preceding settled transaction.
-- The window includes not_before and excludes before.
-- A request before not_before can retry with the same key.
-- Before guards observe the original state.
-- After guards observe the candidate containing the ordered then operations.
-- The otherwise operations start from the original state when a guard fails.
-- TxTime and TxBasis in selected rows name the receipt's transaction time and visible basis.
-- Unchanged checks a sealed branch, basis, layer and footprint.
-- A branch-wide footprint conservatively covers row changes, changes to shared declarations and valid-time boundaries.
-- A transaction applies the selected effects and receipt atomically.
-- The ordered receipt records Then, Otherwise with its failed guard positions, or Refused with its error.

rule ValidateRtdbV1 {
    when: TransactValidationRequested(request, reader)
    requires: LinearizablePrefix(reader, basis)
    ensures:
        ValidationAnswered(request, ResolveGuards(request, basis), basis)
}

rule PublishRtdbV1Transaction {
    when: UntimedTxReadBack(tx, basis)
    ensures:
        ResolvedEffectsApplied(tx, basis)
        ReceiptAvailable(tx, basis)
}

rule StageRtdbV1TimedTransaction {
    when: TimedTxReadBack(tx, lsn)
    ensures:
        TimedTransactionPending(tx, lsn)
}

rule SettleRtdbV1TimedTransaction {
    -- The original writer observes durable acknowledgement before the cutoff and orders Settle in the adjacent slot.
    -- The adjacent slot supplies the TxBasis used to resolve row placeholders.
    when: SettleReadBack(tx_lsn, basis)
    requires: PendingTimedTransaction(tx_lsn)
    ensures:
        ResolvedEffectsApplied(PendingAt(tx_lsn), basis)
        ReceiptAvailable(PendingAt(tx_lsn), basis)
}

rule RefuseRtdbV1TimedTransaction {
    -- A writer on takeover orders Refuse for the recovered pending record before admitting another transaction.
    -- The same writer orders Refuse if it cannot attest timely original durability.
    when: RefuseReadBack(tx_lsn, basis)
    requires: PendingTimedTransaction(tx_lsn)
    ensures:
        RefusedReceiptAvailable(PendingAt(tx_lsn), basis)
}

invariant TimedEffectsFollowSettle {
    for tx in TimedTransactions:
        EffectsVisible(tx) implies Settled(tx)
}

---- Open Questions

open question "AutoCommitDml stamps opts with user and default_tz only. Is the read basis (await_token) the right post-write visibility contract for the immediately-following auto-commit query, or should auto-commit DML and the following query share a tighter basis?"

open question "Should pgwire cancellation require the BackendKeyData secret as well as the connection identifier?"
    -- The pinned frontend sends a zero secret and routes CancelRequest by connection identifier alone.

open question "Which Flight SQL sessions may use a transaction or prepared-statement handle?"
    -- FlightSqlTransaction describes a transaction reachable from any session.
    -- At c84f9334eb060ceb92d28ecdd4a06e6b625932fd, XtdbProducer compares the handle's owner with the calling session's Caller identity.
    -- Authenticated callers are session-specific, while anonymous sessions share one Caller.
